window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][5] = "<p>I'd come back to the CEO within a week with a one-page answer: which segments are weaker, how much, and what the weakest segment says. If the data shows retention is healthy, I'd say so, and ask what else prompted the concern, because the signal may be about something I haven't measured, such as word of mouth.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> The CEO says it was a competitor's product that made them feel this. How do you proceed? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would turn the comparison into specific questions: what does the competitor do better, for which users and tasks, and what do our users who also use it say? I would check whether our retention differs among users who use both products, and read reviews that mention the competitor. I would not copy their feature list; I would find the job where they win and test whether it matters for our segment. Then I would report back with the segment, the problem and one test, so the CEO sees the signal turned into a decision.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you bring the answer back to the CEO in a week? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;With a one-page answer first: the segment that retains and recommends at lower rates than we expect, the evidence (cohort retention and the survey), what interviews say is missing, and the proposed decision for next quarter's roadmap. I would state my confidence and what I could not check. I would also say what would change my conclusion. If the signal turned out to be one conversation and the data show no problem, I would say that too and thank them for prompting the check.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c03-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C03-05 · Hard · structuring · PR</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Churn at a B2B product has doubled in two quarters. You have ten minutes. Structure the problem and tell me the first three things you'd check and why.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Lists causes (price, competitors, product, service) and says &quot;look at customer feedback.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Clarifies churn's definition and timing, builds a MECE structure, chooses three checks with reasons.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Checks measurement and renewal timing before causes, uses concentration to decide where to look, orders checks by likelihood and cost, and includes a reversible mitigation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>Clarify (two minutes).</em> Is churn counted by customers or by revenue? Is it logos lost or contracts not renewed? Doubling from what, say from 2% to 4% a quarter (invented)? Was it gradual or a step? Is it concentrated in a segment, a plan or a size? Did anything change: price, packaging, support, product? And what is the decision: where to focus a retention effort?</p>\n<p><em>Structure.</em> Churn is a ratio too: customers lost divided by customers up for renewal. So I'd ask first whether the numerator or the denominator changed, which means checking renewal timing: a large annual cohort renewing this quarter would raise churn without any change in behavior. Then I'd cut by who left (segment and size) and why (cause type: product value, price, competition, service, customer's own situation).</p>\n<p><em>First three checks, with reasons.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Measurement and renewal mix.</strong> Cheap, and it could change the whole story. Compare churn within each renewal cohort rather than in aggregate. If churn within cohorts is flat, the doubling is a timing effect.</li>\n<li><strong>Concentration.</strong> Cheap and high impact. Which segment, plan or size explains most of the lost revenue? If three large accounts explain half of it, the response is different from a broad slide.</li>\n<li><strong>Recent changes.</strong> The release, pricing and support log for the two quarters. A price rise or a plan change would show as churn concentrated in the affected plan after its date.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>Hypotheses.</em> H1: the change is a renewal-mix effect; I'd reject it if churn within each cohort also rose. H2: a pricing or packaging change drove churn in one plan; I'd reject it if the plan's churn rate did not change after the date. H3: competitor pressure in one segment; I'd look for churned accounts that moved to the same alternative.</p>\n<p><em>Conclude.</em> &quot;I'd report which of these holds with a confidence level. In parallel I'd start a reversible mitigation for at-risk accounts in the affected segment: a customer-success call and an offer review for accounts that are close to renewal. I'd change course if the check shows churn is a mix effect, in which case the mitigation is unnecessary and I would put the effort into confirming the forecast for the next quarter.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Churn within each renewal cohort is flat. What do you conclude, and what next? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;That the doubling is a timing effect: a large cohort came up for renewal this quarter, so the aggregate rose without a change in behavior. I would tell leadership that the retention of each cohort is stable, show the renewal calendar for the next four quarters so they can see when large cohorts return, and still check concentration and reasons, since a stable rate can hide a quiet problem in a segment. I would also check that the measure counts customers or revenue consistently, because the conclusion differs if big accounts are driving it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Three large accounts explain half of the lost revenue. What do you do? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The response becomes account-specific, not a broad program. I would find out why each left: a champion who departed, a price or contract issue, a missing capability, a competitor. I would look for early signals we missed, such as falling usage in the months before, and build an account-health view to catch them. I would also check whether other large accounts show the same warning signs and prioritize outreach to them, with a named owner for each and a review date.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c03-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C03-06 · Hard · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Tell me about a time your first diagnosis of a problem was wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> A story in which the candidate was &quot;mostly right&quot; or the error was somebody else's, with no change in behavior.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States the first hypothesis, how it was tested, what showed it was wrong, and what the real cause was.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows what the candidate did to avoid anchoring, how quickly they dropped the hypothesis, what they changed in their working method afterward, and the cost of the error.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>Illustrative: a composite story; numbers invented.</em> &quot;I was the PM for an online store's mobile checkout. Conversion fell from 3.1% to 2.6% over two weeks. My first hypothesis was that a new payment provider we had added caused failures, because the timing matched the release. I was fairly sure, and I told my manager that I thought it was payments.</p>\n<p>&quot;I checked it as a hypothesis I could reject. If payments were failing, payment success rates would have fallen. They had not: success was 97% before and after. I dropped the hypothesis the same day, about six hours after I had stated it.</p>\n<p>&quot;The next check was a cut by traffic source. The drop sat almost entirely in mobile web visitors from a new advertising campaign, which accounted for 30% of traffic in the second week and converted at 0.8% (invented). The overall rate fell because the mix changed, not because checkout worsened. Within each source, conversion was flat.</p>\n<p>&quot;The cost of my first diagnosis was about half a day of an engineer's time, which I had asked to review payment logs. What I changed afterward was my default order of checks. I now check who the traffic was before I check what the product did, and the first question in our weekly metric review is 'what changed in the audience?' I also write my hypothesis down with what would reject it before I look at data, because I noticed I had been looking for confirmation.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How did you avoid anchoring on your first hypothesis? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By turning it into a prediction I could reject. I wrote what payment failure would look like (success rates falling) and checked that first, which took minutes. Stating the hypothesis aloud to my manager felt risky, but it made me accountable for checking it. I also asked what else changed in the same two weeks, which opened the traffic-mix question. The habit I keep is to write the rejection criterion before looking at the data.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What did the wrong diagnosis cost, and how did you manage it with your team? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;About half a day of one engineer's time reviewing payment logs, which I told the engineer honestly: I had been wrong and the log review was still useful because it ruled out a cause. I said so in the weekly metrics review without defensiveness, and I changed the default order of checks for everyone: traffic mix first, then product changes. Owning the miss early made it easier for others to say when they had been wrong.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c03-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C03-07 · Beginner · structuring · PR</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Support tickets have doubled in three months, and the head of support wants to hire more agents. How do you think about it?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Agrees to hire, or lists possible causes with no structure and no numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Splits tickets into users times contact rate, looks at categories, separates growth in users from a higher rate, and compares the options by cost and time to take effect.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats hiring as a bridge, ties each cause to a cheap check, sizes what fixing the top cause would save in agent time, and says what would make hiring the right call.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I would first ask whether the number of users or the rate of tickets per user doubled, because they point to different problems. Tickets are users times the contact rate. Suppose tickets rose from 20,000 to 40,000 a month while active users rose from 500,000 to 550,000 (invented). The contact rate went from 40 to 72.7 tickets per 1,000 users, so almost all of the doubling is a higher rate, not growth.</p>\n<p>Next, where the tickets come from. By category, suppose billing questions rose from 5,000 to 17,000 and everything else from 15,000 to 23,000 (invented). Billing explains 12,000 of the 20,000 extra tickets, 60%, so it is the first place to look.</p>\n<p>Then I would ask which cause family fits billing: a product or policy change near the start of the rise (a new pricing page, a billing release), a confusing flow, an incident that left a backlog, a change in how tickets are counted, or a seasonal peak. The cheapest check is the date the billing tickets began to rise against the release and pricing calendar, then a read of 50 billing tickets for the common complaint.</p>\n<p>Options. Hiring: 20,000 extra tickets at 12 minutes each is 4,000 hours a month; at about 105 productive hours per agent that is 38 agents, and recruiting and training take about two months. Fixing the top cause could remove up to 12,000 tickets, which is 2,400 hours, or 23 agents' worth of work. So I would fix the billing cause, add temporary capacity for the weeks in between, and set a hiring trigger: if the contact rate stays above 50 per 1,000 users after the fix, the rest is real demand and hiring is right.</p>\n<p>I would change my mind if the rate rose in every category, which points to a product quality problem or a counting change, or if the billing tickets turned out to be unavoidable, such as required notices. Then more agents are the correct answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you know the fix worked? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Billing tickets per 1,000 users falling toward the earlier level within two to four weeks of the fix, with resolution time and satisfaction not worse. I would also watch for tickets moving to another category instead of disappearing.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> The head of support says morale is falling and you cannot wait. What do you do? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Separate the urgent from the structural. Approve temporary capacity now, such as contractors or overtime, sized to the backlog and with an end date tied to the fix, and tell the head of support the plan and the metric. Permanent hires for a spike that a fix will remove would leave the team over capacity later.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c03-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C03-08 · Intermediate · structuring · PR</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Your team can build a messaging service in-house or buy one from a vendor that charges per message. How do you structure the decision?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Lists pros and cons (&quot;build gives control, buy is faster&quot;) with no criteria and no numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the criteria (cost over time, time to market, strategic importance, risk), compares costs at the expected volume, and recommends with a condition.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Finds the break-even volume, tests the decision against volume uncertainty and reversibility, counts the engineers' time, and proposes a staged path (buy now, build at a trigger).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I would structure it on four criteria: cost over three years, time to market, how strategic the capability is, and risk and reversibility. Cost first, with invented inputs. The vendor charges $0.01 a message. Building costs $2,000,000 once, four engineers to maintain it at $200,000 each ($800,000 a year), and infrastructure of $0.001 a message. Over 36 months, buying costs 0.01 × messages a month × 36 and building costs $2,000,000 + 3 × $800,000 + 0.001 × messages a month × 36.</p>\n<p>At 8 million messages a month (today, invented) buying costs $2,880,000 and building $4,688,000, so buying is cheaper by $1,808,000. At 14 million the two are about equal ($5,040,000 against $4,904,000). At 30 million building is cheaper by $5,320,000. The break-even is $4,400,000 ÷ (($0.01 − $0.001) × 36) = about 13.6 million messages a month.</p>\n<p>The other criteria. Time: the vendor is live in about four weeks and a build takes about nine months (invented), which matters if messaging gates a launch. Strategic importance: if messaging is not what customers choose us for, building it is a distraction. Reversibility: buying is reversible if the product talks to the vendor through our own interface; building is not.</p>\n<p>My recommendation: buy now, behind an interface, because today's volume is below break-even and speed matters. Set a trigger to revisit: sustained volume above about 14 million a month for two quarters, or messaging becoming a differentiator, or vendor terms or reliability failing. I would change my mind if volume were already near 30 million or if the vendor could not meet a requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the vendor raises its price by 50%? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The price becomes $0.015, and the break-even falls to $4,400,000 ÷ (($0.015 − $0.001) × 36) = about 8.7 million messages a month, close to today's 8 million. A price rise pulls the build decision forward, so I would negotiate price protection and prepare the interface and a migration estimate now.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you keep the decision reversible? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Put an interface between the product and the vendor, keep message data in our own store, and write down the cost and weeks of switching. The exit plan is what makes buying low-risk.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c03-09\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C03-09 · Intermediate · structuring · PR</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Leadership sets a goal to double weekly active users in a year. How do you structure the problem of how to get there?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Lists growth ideas (more marketing, new features) with no structure and no numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Decomposes weekly actives into additions and losses, checks what the goal requires, and ranks levers by size and cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the steady-state relationship between monthly additions and the loss rate to show what doubling requires, tests whether one lever can do it, and challenges the definition and the purpose of the goal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I would start by writing weekly actives as a stock with flows. Suppose today there are 1,000,000 weekly actives (invented). Each month about 80,000 users are added (new and returning) and about 6% of the base, 60,000, drops out. Net growth is +20,000 a month. A base like this drifts toward a steady state of additions divided by the loss rate: 80,000 ÷ 0.06 = 1,333,333. After 12 months the base would be about 1,174,693, roughly 17% up, not double.</p>\n<p>What doubling requires. To reach 2,000,000 in 12 months with the same 6% loss rate, additions must be about 174,486 a month, 2.2 times today's. Cutting the loss rate from 6% to 4% with additions unchanged gets to only 1,387,290. Cutting the loss rate to 4% and raising additions to 150,000 gets to 2,065,048. So doubling needs both levers at once, with additions up by nearly two thirds more than today's and the loss rate down by a third (the combination of 150,000 additions and 4% loss, the numbers invented).</p>\n<p>Then the questions about the goal. What counts as active (one visit or one core action)? A looser definition could double the number without doubling the value. What is the number for: revenue, a market position, a story for investors? That decides whether retention (cheaper, compounds) or acquisition (faster, costly) should lead.</p>\n<p>So I would propose intermediate targets by lever, for example first-month loss, additions by channel and returning users, test the cheapest lever first, and report each quarter against the path the arithmetic implies. If leadership keeps the target, I would show them plainly that it needs about 1.9 times today's additions and a third lower loss rate together.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Which lever would you start with? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Retention of new cohorts, if most of the loss happens in the first weeks. It compounds, because each user kept stays in the base for later months, and it is usually cheaper than buying users. I would check the loss by cohort age first to see where it concentrates, and test an onboarding change before any spend.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if leadership insists on doubling anyway? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would put the arithmetic in front of them: additions up by almost 90% and the loss rate down by a third, both within a year. Then I would propose staged targets, ask what the number is for, and agree what would tell us in three months whether the path is realistic. I would not hide that the target needs both levers.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c03-10\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C03-10 · Hard · structuring · PR</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Trust and safety wants an ID check at sign-up to stop fraud. Growth says the check will cost sign-ups. How do you decide?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Picks a side, or says &quot;find a balance&quot;, with no numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Puts both costs in one unit, estimates each from stated assumptions, and compares at least two options (check everyone, check no one) before deciding.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Finds the option between the extremes (a risk-based check), computes its value, names the assumption that decides it, proposes a test with guardrails, and says who decides and when to revisit.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I would put both sides in one unit, dollars a month, with invented inputs. Suppose 100,000 sign-ups a month, 3% of them fraudulent (3,000), each fraudulent account costing $60, and each legitimate sign-up worth $25 of contribution. The check stops 90% of fraud and makes 15% of legitimate users give up.</p>\n<p>Check everyone: fraud stopped is 2,700 × $60 = $162,000; legitimate users lost are 14,550 × $25 = $363,750. Net: −$201,750 a month. Check no one: nothing gained or lost against today. So a blanket check loses money on these numbers, and the answer to &quot;safety or growth&quot; is neither extreme.</p>\n<p>Between them: a risk-based check. Suppose a risk score sends the riskiest 20,000 sign-ups (20%) to the check, and that group holds 70% of the fraud: 2,100 fraudulent and 17,900 legitimate. The check stops 1,890 fraudulent accounts, worth $113,400, and costs 2,685 legitimate users, worth $67,125. Net: +$46,275 a month.</p>\n<p>The assumption that decides it is how many legitimate users in the risky group give up. At 30% instead of 15%, the net becomes −$20,850. So I would run it as a staged test with a holdout, with guardrails on legitimate sign-up completion, support contacts and fraud losses, and expand only if the net is positive with its interval. Product, trust and safety and growth decide together on that rule, and I would review it monthly, because fraudsters adapt.</p>\n<p>Two things sit outside the arithmetic: harm to people that a dollar figure understates, and legal or partner requirements, which can make the check mandatory in some markets.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if legal says the check is required? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then the question is how to make it cheapest, not whether to do it. Reuse an existing verified identity where allowed, move the check to after the first value if the rules permit it, and measure the drop-off at each step to remove friction. I would also tell growth the expected cost in advance.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you handle fraudsters adapting to the risk score? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Treat the score as a moving target. Watch the fraud rate within the unchecked 80% every week, retrain the score on confirmed cases, and keep a small random sample of unscored sign-ups going through the check so that I can measure what the score misses.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c03-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Numbers are invented.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c03-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C03-01 · MECE or not</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> For each split, say whether it is MECE, state the failure, and give a fix: (a) customers: enterprise, small business, mobile users; (b) revenue: visits × conversion rate × average order value; (c) causes of low sales pipeline: product, marketing, sales, other; (d) monthly users: new, returning, churned; (e) devices: iOS, Android, tablets, web.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You are building trees for a B2B and consumer portfolio.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None beyond the lists.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> One sentence per item.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Apply the overlap, gap, size and level tests (FW-C03-02).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Not MECE: &quot;mobile users&quot; is a device, not a customer size, and overlaps both others. Fix: enterprise, mid-market, small business. (b) MECE by construction; every change in revenue is a change in one factor. (c) Not MECE: the first three are functions, &quot;other&quot; is likely large, and causes are not functions. Fix: split by funnel stage (awareness, interest, qualification, close) or by cause type (demand, targeting, execution). (d) MECE if status is defined at a date (new: first active this month; returning: active before and now; churned: active before and not now); note that churned users are not &quot;monthly users&quot; in the strict sense, so define the whole accurately. (e) Not MECE: tablets overlap iOS and Android, and form factor is a different dimension from operating system. Fix: choose one dimension (operating system or form factor).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Test each with a concrete example: &quot;an iPad user&quot;: which branch? If two, it overlaps.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Fixing every overlap costs time; fix those that would put evidence in the wrong branch.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c03-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C03-02 · Choose the cut</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A subscription app's revenue fell 6% last month. For each decision, choose the MECE cut that serves it: (a) where the growth team should focus; (b) whether to change the price; (c) whether to change the plan structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You can use any standard cut.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Revenue = active subscribers × average revenue per subscriber (invented structure).</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Say why each cut serves the decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> For each decision, ask: if the answer were branch A versus B, what would I do?</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Cut by acquisition channel: the action differs by channel (which to invest in). (b) Cut revenue as subscribers × average revenue per subscriber: if subscribers fell but price per subscriber held, a price change is the wrong lever; if average revenue per subscriber fell, price or mix matters. (c) Cut by plan mix: if subscribers moved to a cheaper plan, the structure is the issue. Each cut is MECE and leads to a different action.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Two-level: first subscribers × revenue per subscriber, then channel within subscribers and plan mix within revenue per subscriber.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> The two-level tree is more complete and slower; use it when time allows.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c03-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C03-03 · Build and score a tree</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Fewer new users complete profile setup. Build a scored issue tree and order the checks.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Setup has four steps.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> <em>Invented.</em> Of 100 users who start: 85 finish name and photo, 60 finish interests, 52 connect contacts, 50 finish.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Two levels deep; one page.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Choose a funnel cut, compute losses, score, check arithmetic.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Losses per 100 starts: name and photo 15, interests 25, contacts 8, final step 2; total 50. Interests holds half of all losses. Branches: interests step (high likelihood, high impact, low cost to check, order 1); name and photo (medium, medium, low, 2); contacts (low, low, low, 3); final (low, low, low, 4); instrumentation (low, high, low, check once). Hypotheses for interests: too many required selections; unclear value; poor mobile layout. Checks: time on screen, required-selection count versus drop, session recordings. The loss figures sum to 50 and completion is 50%, matching the data.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a segment cut (new platform versus others) if the funnel cut shows no concentration.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A funnel cut hides audience differences; add a segment look after the first check.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c03-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C03-04 · Write hypotheses that can fail</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Rewrite each into a hypothesis card with claim, because, we would also expect, reject if: (a) &quot;Retention is bad.&quot; (b) &quot;Users don't like the new design.&quot; (c) &quot;Notifications are annoying.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You are a PM with event and survey data.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None specified.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Each claim must name a segment or a mechanism.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use FW-C03-04 and make each claim falsifiable.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Claim: &quot;Week-four retention of users who did not complete setup is at least 15 points lower than for those who did.&quot; Because: setup gives first value. Also expect: the gap is largest in the first two weeks. Reject if the gap is under 5 points. (b) Claim: &quot;Task completion on the settings screen fell after the redesign.&quot; Because: controls were moved. Also expect: more taps to reach the same setting. Reject if completion and taps are unchanged. (c) Claim: &quot;Users who receive more than three notifications a day turn them off at twice the rate of those who receive one.&quot; Because: frequency causes fatigue. Also expect: opt-out concentrated in high-frequency cohorts. Reject if opt-out rates are similar across frequency.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a cost to check and an action if true to each card.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Specific claims may be too narrow; run several competing claims.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c03-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C03-05 · Prune and prioritize</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Order these leaves for checking: A (likelihood high, impact high, cost high), B (high, medium, low), C (low, high, low), D (medium, high, medium), E (low, low, high), F (high, high, low).</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A diagnostic tree for a drop in repeat purchases.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As given.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Apply the ordering rule in FW-C03-03 and name what you would drop.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> High likelihood and low cost first; high likelihood and high cost second; low likelihood and low cost third; low likelihood and high cost last.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Order: F (high likelihood, high impact, low cost), B (high, medium, low), A (high, high, high cost), D (medium) before C? The rule puts low likelihood below medium; D (medium, high, medium cost) is next, then C (low likelihood but low cost, so a quick check), and E last or dropped (low likelihood, low impact, high cost). So: F, B, A, D, C, then drop E. Note that A is expensive and high-impact, so start it early in parallel if resources allow.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Order by expected value (likelihood × impact ÷ cost) with numbers 1 to 3, and compare with the rule.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Numeric scoring invites false precision; use it only to break ties.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c03-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C03-06 · Five whys and a fault tree</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A release shipped with a broken password-reset email for two days. Apply the five whys and a fault tree.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Facts: the reset link used a hard-coded web address; staging used a different address, so tests passed; the release checklist had no reset test on production-like settings; monitoring did not track reset completion; the release went out on a Friday.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As listed.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Name corrective actions that do not blame a person.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Run a five-whys chain and then a fault tree with AND and OR.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Five whys: (1) Why did users not receive working links? The link pointed to the wrong address. (2) Why? The address was hard-coded. (3) Why was it not caught? Staging used a different address, so tests passed. (4) Why was it not caught later? No test on production-like settings, and no monitoring of reset completion. (5) Why? The release checklist had no such step. Fault tree: outage requires the defect AND no test catching it AND no monitor catching it; the Friday timing is a contributing factor in the duration. Actions: remove hard-coded addresses; add a production-like test; add a monitor on reset completion; avoid unmonitored Friday releases for authentication changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a fishbone for contributing causes by category (process, tooling, people, timing).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> More actions reduce risk and add process; choose those that cut the most cause at the least cost.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c03-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C03-07 · Symptom or cause</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Classify each statement as a symptom, a proximate cause or a root cause, and explain: (a) &quot;Conversion fell.&quot; (b) &quot;The new checkout button is hidden on small phones.&quot; (c) &quot;The design review did not test small screens.&quot; (d) &quot;Support tickets doubled.&quot; (e) &quot;A caching change returned stale prices.&quot; (f) &quot;There is no process to compare caching changes against price data.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Two incidents are mixed together.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As listed.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> One sentence each.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Apply the symptom test: does fixing it stop recurrence?</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Symptom. (b) Proximate cause of the conversion fall. (c) Contributing or root cause (the process gap that let it ship). (d) Symptom. (e) Proximate cause of stale prices and the ticket rise. (f) Root cause (missing control). Fixing (b) corrects the instance; fixing (c) and (f) prevents recurrence.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Draw the two chains separately and mark where they would branch.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A deeper root cause is not always worth fixing; weigh the cost of recurrence.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c03-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C03-08 · Structure a decision</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Should a note-taking app localize into a new language?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Engineering says localization takes eight weeks (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> <em>Invented.</em> 14% of signups come from the language's region, with 40% lower activation than average; search shows rising interest; a competitor has localized.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Use a Type B decision structure and make a recommendation with the cost stated.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Value, feasibility, viability, risk and reversibility.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Value: 14% of signups with low activation suggests latent demand blocked by language (hypothesis, not proof). Feasibility: eight weeks of engineering plus ongoing translation. Viability: depends on willingness to pay in the region; check pricing. Risk: low quality translation can harm trust; it is mostly reversible but a localized product creates expectations. Options: full localization; localize the first-run experience only; do nothing. Recommendation: localize onboarding and core screens first (about three weeks, invented), giving up full coverage to test whether activation rises. Expand if activation in the region improves by a set amount. Change the view if activation does not move.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Run a fake-door test showing a &quot;coming soon in your language&quot; prompt and count clicks.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A partial localization is faster and may feel unfinished to users.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c03-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C03-09 · Three-minute structuring drill</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> In three minutes, structure: &quot;Why would users stop using a note-taking app after a month?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A timed spoken drill.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Frame, structure, prioritize in 180 seconds.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Frame the metric; choose a lifecycle cut; name two branches to check first.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Frame (20 seconds): the question is why monthly retention falls after week four, and the decision is which stage to fix; assume it is lower than for similar products. Structure (60 seconds) by the user lifecycle: never reached value (setup and first notes), reached value but no habit (no trigger to return), switching or migration (found another tool, or sync issues), and changed circumstances (project ended). Prioritize (40 seconds): check what share of lapsed users created more than five notes; if most did not, the problem is early value; if they did, the problem is habit or switching. Say what you would do in each case. Conclude (60 seconds): name the first check and the reversible action.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Cut by segment (students, professionals) first if the product has distinct audiences.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Three minutes allows breadth, not depth; say what you would do with more time.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c03-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C03-10 · Spot false completeness</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A team's tree for &quot;why did app ratings fall?&quot; has four branches: bugs, slow performance, competitors, other. In the data, 60% of low ratings fall under &quot;other.&quot; Critique and fix the tree.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The team was proud of the tree.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Reasons were coded from 1,000 one-star reviews (invented): bugs 20%, performance 15%, competitors 5%, other 60%; re-reading the &quot;other&quot; reviews shows that 15 points of the 60 are about price.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Show the revised tree and say what the first check is.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Apply the size test and the level test.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> &quot;Other&quot; is 60% and price (15 points of it) is hiding inside it, so the tree has false completeness. Bugs and performance are product quality; competitors is an external factor; they are not at the same level. Revise by cause type: product quality (bugs, performance), value and price, service and support, expectations set by marketing, external factors (competitors). Re-code &quot;other&quot; into these, then look at what remains. First check: which of the new categories grew in the period (ratings are a change over time, not a snapshot).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Cluster the &quot;other&quot; reviews by topic with a simple keyword pass and look at the top five themes.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Re-coding takes time; a sample of 100 &quot;other&quot; reviews is usually enough to reveal missing branches.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for three weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: structure only.</em> Pick a prompt (use the exercises and IQ-C03-01 to IQ-C03-10). Set a three-minute timer. Frame, choose a cut, give three to five branches, and name the two you would check first. Stop at three minutes.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: the skeleton.</em> Use the ten-minute budget in section 4 on a diagnostic prompt, and speak aloud or record.</li>\n<li><em>Week 3: add data.</em> Ask a partner to provide a data point at minute five and to redirect you once. Practice updating aloud.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>After every drill (three minutes).</strong> Redraw your tree cleanly on one page and run the MECE cut tests on it. The redraw is where most of the learning happens.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I clarify before structuring, and did I ask no more than five questions?</li>\n<li>Did I say the decision behind the question?</li>\n<li>Did I name the cut and why it serves the decision?</li>\n<li>Do my branches pass the overlap and gap tests?</li>\n<li>Is &quot;other&quot; small, or did I name what I excluded?</li>\n<li>Did I prioritize and give a reason?</li>\n<li>Did I state at least one hypothesis with a rejection condition?</li>\n<li>Did I say what a result rules out?</li>\n<li>Did I separate the symptom from the cause?</li>\n<li>Did I end with a belief, a confidence level and a next action?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to interrupt once with &quot;what would that rule out?&quot; and once with a new fact that contradicts your leading hypothesis. Note whether you update or defend.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the prompt, the cut you chose, your score out of ten, the item you will fix, and the cut you would try next time. After six drills, check which cuts you reach for most. If it is always the same one, practice a different structure from the library (FW-C03-06).</p>\n<span id=\"c03-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter in the next two weeks on real work. Each deliverable doubles as an interview story.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 2.</strong> Choose a live ambiguity: a metric dip, a recurring complaint, or a vague request. Write a frame card (FW-C03-01) and show it to the person who raised it; revise until they agree it captures the question and the decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 3 to 5.</strong> Build a scored issue tree (FW-C03-03). Run the MECE cut tests. Ask a colleague to place three real examples in the tree and tell you where they do not fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Write hypothesis cards (FW-C03-04) for your top two branches, run the cheapest checks, and record what each rules out. Write a one-page memo using the decision-memo template from C02 (FW-C02-06) with the finding, the confidence, the action and what would change it.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Run a five-whys, and then a fault tree, on one recent incident or miss from your team. Propose corrective actions that change a process, not a person.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the frame card and tree (a time you brought structure to ambiguity), the memo (a decision with a stated confidence), and the incident analysis (a time you found the cause behind a symptom). Keep the numbers real.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c03-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Clarify and frame before you structure: the decision behind the question decides the cut.</li>\n<li>A MECE cut is only useful if the branches lead to different actions; choose the cut that serves the decision.</li>\n<li>Prioritize branches by likelihood, impact and cost to check, and state hypotheses that can be rejected.</li>\n<li>Separate symptoms from causes, expect several contributing causes, and when harm is ongoing, mitigate with something reversible before you diagnose.</li>\n<li>Use frameworks for what the question needs; reciting one without using it is theater.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c03-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Barbara Minto, <em>The Pyramid Principle</em> (multiple editions). The source of MECE-style structured thinking and of the practice of leading with the answer.</li>\n<li>George Pólya, <em>How to Solve It</em> (1945). A classic account of problem-solving heuristics, including restating the problem and working backward.</li>\n<li>Eric Ries, <em>The Lean Startup</em> (2011). The Build–Measure–Learn loop used in FW-C03-05.</li>\n<li>Taiichi Ohno, <em>Toyota Production System: Beyond Large-Scale Production</em> (English edition 1988). The origin of the five whys.</li>\n<li>Karl Popper, <em>The Logic of Scientific Discovery</em> (English edition 1959). The case for hypotheses that can be falsified.</li>\n<li>Donella Meadows, <em>Thinking in Systems</em> (2008). Useful for the multi-cause, feedback-driven failures that single-cause analysis misses.</li>\n<li>Ethan Rasiel, <em>The McKinsey Way</em> (1999). A short, readable account of structured problem solving in consulting.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c04\"></span>\n<h2>C04 — Estimation Under Pressure</h2>\n<span id=\"c04-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>The skill is producing a reasoned approximate number, quickly, from assumptions you state, and showing that the number is good enough for the decision it serves. C03 trained you to structure a problem. Estimation is structure applied to a quantity: you split an unknown into factors you can reason about, put a number on each, and check the result.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: a dedicated estimation round in some early-career loops (reported as program-specific for Google's APM program), estimation prompts inside product and strategy answers where you size a market or an opportunity, and capacity or cost questions in technical rounds. On the job the same skill appears when you size an opportunity before you prioritize it, when you plan capacity or cost with engineers, when you forecast launch demand, and when you check a number on someone else's slide that looks too good.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Google publicly lists general cognitive ability among its hiring attributes <code>Public</code> <code>[V: summaries citing Google's how-we-hire page; Google re:Work structured-interviewing guide]</code>, and an estimation prompt is a compact way to observe it <code>Inferred</code>. Amazon publishes principles that include Dive Deep and Are Right, A Lot <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon's Leadership Principles page, amazon.jobs]</code>, and candidates commonly report follow-ups that test whether you checked a number <code>Inferred</code>. The scorer is not looking for the &quot;right&quot; answer, because there rarely is one. The scorer is looking for five behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You clarify what is being estimated and why.</li>\n<li>You decompose it into factors you can estimate.</li>\n<li>You state each assumption aloud, with a reason, in round numbers.</li>\n<li>You check the result by a second route and say which assumption dominates it.</li>\n<li>You connect the number to a decision and say how wrong it could be.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Five patterns lose candidates and new PMs: guessing a final number with no path to it, reciting memorized &quot;facts&quot; that may be wrong, false precision (an answer of 4,127,500), arithmetic that collapses under time pressure, and a correct estimate that is never connected to a decision.</p>\n<span id=\"c04-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c04-c04-1-what-estimation-is-and-why-it-works\"></span>\n<h4>C04.1 What estimation is, and why it works</h4>\n<p><strong>Estimation</strong> is producing a reasoned approximate number from stated assumptions and a decomposition of the quantity. <a href=\"#gl-estimation\">estimation</a> is sometimes called a Fermi problem, after the physicist Enrico Fermi, who was known for quick order-of-magnitude estimates from simple assumptions <code>[V: Fermi's Trinity estimate, CS-C04-02]</code>.</p>\n<p>Two properties make it work.</p>\n<p><strong>Decomposition turns one unknowable guess into several guessable ones.</strong> You cannot guess the number of coffee shops in a city of two million people, but you can guess how many people buy coffee out on a typical day and how many customers one shop serves. Each guess is closer to something you have seen.</p>\n<p><strong>Independent errors partly cancel.</strong> If you guess one factor too high and another too low, the product is closer than either error alone. The multiplicative error of a product of independent factors is usually smaller than the worst case, which is why a decomposition into four rough factors is often within a factor of two or three of the truth <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n<p>The product of the steps is therefore only as good as the weakest factor, and the skill is knowing which factor that is. That is <a href=\"#gl-sensitivity-analysis\">sensitivity analysis</a> (C04.5).</p>\n<p><strong>When not to estimate.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>When you can look it up or measure it in less time than you would spend estimating: a count in your own database is a ten-minute query, not a guess.</li>\n<li>When the decision does not depend on the number within a wide range. If the action is the same at one million and at ten million, the estimate adds nothing.</li>\n<li>When accuracy is required, as in financial reporting, safety margins or contract terms.</li>\n<li>When the quantity is dominated by a few large values, so that a product of averages misleads.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A team estimates revenue from a customer base as customers times average revenue per customer. But 2% of customers produce 60% of revenue (invented). The average is a poor guide for the segment the product actually serves, and the product of two averages produces a number that describes no real customer. The right move is to segment first (large and small customers separately) and estimate each. Decomposition works when the factors are roughly uniform; when they are heavy-tailed, split the population before you multiply.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risk.</strong> A tidy decomposition can feel more reliable than it is. Four precise-looking factors can hide a bad assumption about one of them, and the confidence a clean calculation produces is not evidence.</p>\n<span id=\"c04-c04-2-the-method-clarify-decompose-assume-compute-sanity-che\"></span>\n<h4>C04.2 The method: clarify, decompose, assume, compute, sanity-check</h4>\n<p>Five steps, in this order. FW-C04-01 records the protocol.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Clarify.</strong> Pin down what is being estimated (the definition, the unit, the place and the time), whether it is a stock (how many exist now) or a flow (how many per day), and why it is being asked. Ask two or three questions. If the interviewer says &quot;you decide,&quot; state your definition aloud.</li>\n<li><strong>Decompose.</strong> Choose a structure from the library in C04.4 and write three or four factors that multiply or add to the answer. Each factor must be something you can reason about. Use the logic of <a href=\"#gl-mece\">MECE</a> (C03): factors should not overlap, and together they should cover the quantity.</li>\n<li><strong>Assume.</strong> Give each factor a number, a reason, and a provenance (a defined fact, a published figure, or your judgment). Use round numbers: 2, 3, 5, 10, 25, 50. Say each aloud.</li>\n<li><strong>Compute.</strong> Multiply step by step, tracking units and powers of ten. Round at each step to one or two significant figures. Narrate the arithmetic so the interviewer can follow and correct you.</li>\n<li><strong>Sanity-check.</strong> A <strong>sanity check</strong> is testing a result against an independent route or a known bound before trusting it. <a href=\"#gl-sanity-check\">sanity check</a> means a second decomposition, a comparison with something you know, and a check of units. Then say which assumption dominates and what you would do with the number (C04.6).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What to say aloud.</strong> &quot;I'll estimate the number of riders per day. I'll define a rider as someone who takes at least one trip. I'll split by population, share who ride, and trips per rider.&quot; &quot;I'll assume about 70% of people are adults, because roughly a quarter of the population is under 15.&quot; &quot;That gives about 280,000 riders. Let me cross-check from the driver side.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Why the order matters.</strong> Candidates who start with numbers cannot be scored on structure, and those who stop after computing have not shown that they would trust the result. The check and the so-what are the two steps most candidates skip, and they are the two that distinguish Strong answers from Baseline ones.</p>\n<span id=\"c04-c04-3-the-anchor-library-with-provenance-marked\"></span>\n<h4>C04.3 The anchor library, with provenance marked</h4>\n<p>An <strong>anchor</strong> is a well-known quantity used as the starting point for an estimate. <a href=\"#gl-anchor\">anchor</a> is what you reason from. You are not expected to know exact figures. You are expected to pick reasonable ones, state them as assumptions, and say how confident you are.</p>\n<p>Mark where each anchor comes from. FW-C04-03 gives the ladder; the library below shows the three provenance classes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>D</strong> (defined): exact by definition or arithmetic.</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong> (published, approximate): a widely published figure, rounded; verify before relying on it for anything beyond an interview.</li>\n<li><strong>J</strong> (judgment): a figure you reason to from experience; state it as an assumption and give a range.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Group</th>\n<th>Anchor</th>\n<th>Value to use</th>\n<th>Class</th>\n<th>Note</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Time</td>\n<td>Seconds per day</td>\n<td>86,400 (about 10^5)</td>\n<td>D</td>\n<td>10^5 is about 15% too high; fine for rough work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time</td>\n<td>Hours per year</td>\n<td>8,760 (about 9,000)</td>\n<td>D</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time</td>\n<td>Seconds per year</td>\n<td>about 3 × 10^7 (31.5 million)</td>\n<td>D</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time</td>\n<td>Working hours per year</td>\n<td>about 2,000</td>\n<td>J</td>\n<td>40 hours × 50 weeks, both assumed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>People</td>\n<td>World population</td>\n<td>about 8.3 billion</td>\n<td>P <code>[V: UN World Population Prospects 2024, mid-2026]</code></td>\n<td>The 8 billion mark was announced for late 2022</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>People</td>\n<td>India, China</td>\n<td>India about 1.5 billion, China about 1.4 billion</td>\n<td>P <code>[V: UN World Population Prospects 2024, India 1.48 billion for mid-2026]</code></td>\n<td>India passed China around 2023 (the UN announced April 2023; some estimates based on its 2024 edition place it in 2022)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>People</td>\n<td>United States</td>\n<td>about 347 million (2025)</td>\n<td>P <code>[V: UN World Population Prospects 2024: 347 million in 2025]</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>People</td>\n<td>Indonesia, Brazil, Nigeria</td>\n<td>about 285, 215 and 240 million</td>\n<td>P <code>[V: UN World Population Prospects 2024 (INED reproduction): 286, 213 and 238 million in 2025]</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>People</td>\n<td>Large city</td>\n<td>5 to 20 million</td>\n<td>J</td>\n<td>Define by metro area or city proper</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>People</td>\n<td>People per household</td>\n<td>about 2.5 (United States), about 4 to 5 (India)</td>\n<td>P <code>[V: NFHS-5 for India, about 4.4; US Census Bureau, 2.59 in 2010 with one-person households at 29% in 2025]</code></td>\n<td>Varies by country and by year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>People</td>\n<td>Share of the world online</td>\n<td>about three-quarters (74% in 2025)</td>\n<td>P <code>[V: ITU statistics page, 2025: about 6 billion people, 74 per cent of the world's population, online]</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>People</td>\n<td>Share of people under 15</td>\n<td>about a quarter worldwide; much lower in aging countries</td>\n<td>P <code>[V: UN World Population Prospects 2024 as compiled by secondary sites: 25.8 percent aged 0 to 14 worldwide in 2024 (Gitnux) and 16.0 percent in China in 2024 (World Bank series built on UN data); the UN table itself was not opened]</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Devices</td>\n<td>Adults with a smartphone</td>\n<td>70% to 95% depending on market</td>\n<td>J <code>[unverified: judgment range; no source]</code></td>\n<td>State as an assumption</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data</td>\n<td>A phone photo</td>\n<td>about 3 MB</td>\n<td>J</td>\n<td>Range 2 to 5 MB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data</td>\n<td>A minute of phone video</td>\n<td>about 100 MB</td>\n<td>J</td>\n<td>Depends on resolution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data</td>\n<td>Streaming video</td>\n<td>about 1 to 5 Mbps</td>\n<td>J</td>\n<td>Depends on quality tier</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data</td>\n<td>A text message</td>\n<td>140 bytes</td>\n<td>D</td>\n<td>The original SMS limit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Money</td>\n<td>A coffee, a city ride</td>\n<td>$4, $10 to $20</td>\n<td>J</td>\n<td>State the currency and market</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Money</td>\n<td>A monthly streaming subscription</td>\n<td>$10 to $20</td>\n<td>J</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Money</td>\n<td>A smartphone</td>\n<td>$300 to $1,000</td>\n<td>J</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Behavior</td>\n<td>Sleep, waking hours</td>\n<td>8 hours, 16 hours</td>\n<td>J</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Behavior</td>\n<td>Commute</td>\n<td>30 to 60 minutes a day</td>\n<td>J</td>\n<td>Varies widely by city</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Business</td>\n<td>Visit-to-purchase conversion in online retail</td>\n<td>1% to 4%</td>\n<td>J</td>\n<td>Wide range by category</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Business</td>\n<td>Free-to-paid conversion</td>\n<td>2% to 5%</td>\n<td>J</td>\n<td>Wide range by product</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Using the library.</strong> Prefer D to P to J. When you must use a judgment, anchor it to something you know: your own household, your own week. Say &quot;I'm reasonably confident within a factor of two,&quot; and the interviewer hears calibration, not bluff.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Regional Lens: India.</strong> Estimates about India start from a handful of base numbers, so say which you use and when it was measured. The United Nations projects about 1.48 billion people in mid-2026, about 38% of them urban <code>[V: UN World Population Prospects 2024, as reproduced by Worldometer and Statistics Times]</code>; the last completed census was in 2011, so every current figure is a projection. India's telecom regulator counted 1.114 billion internet subscriptions at the end of June 2026 <code>[V: TRAI performance-indicator report, as reported by DD India]</code>. Subscriptions are not people, because one person can hold several, so convert them before treating them as users <code>Inferred</code>. Choose a segment first (C09), and read the current figures yourself at the time you read this book <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c04-c04-4-decomposition-patterns\"></span>\n<h4>C04.4 Decomposition patterns</h4>\n<p>Seven patterns cover most prompts. FW-C04-02 summarizes them, and the table below shows the shape.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Shape</th>\n<th>Use when</th>\n<th>Example prompt</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Top-down (demand side)</td>\n<td>Population × share who qualify × frequency × size</td>\n<td>You know the people better than the supply</td>\n<td>Coffee consumed in a city per day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bottom-up (supply side)</td>\n<td>Units of supply × capacity per unit × utilization</td>\n<td>You know the providers</td>\n<td>Customers served by all coffee shops</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Balance</td>\n<td>Estimate demand and supply, reconcile</td>\n<td>A market with both sides</td>\n<td>Ride-hailing trips</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capacity and throughput</td>\n<td>Arrival rate × time per job ÷ servers; peak versus average</td>\n<td>Systems with queues or limits</td>\n<td>Couriers needed at the dinner peak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Benchmark (reference class)</td>\n<td>Scale a known comparable by a ratio</td>\n<td>A close analog exists</td>\n<td>Orders per capita, scaled from a similar city</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unit economics</td>\n<td>(Revenue − cost) per unit × units</td>\n<td>The question is about profit or payback</td>\n<td>Profit from a new subscription tier</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Funnel</td>\n<td>Starting population × conversion at each step</td>\n<td>A path leads to the outcome</td>\n<td>Paying users from a free product</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Choose a pattern by what you know best,</strong> and use a second pattern for the check. If you reason well about people, use top-down and check from supply. If the prompt is about a system, use capacity.</p>\n<p><strong>Make the factors estimable.</strong> A factor you cannot reason about (&quot;market adoption of an unknown product&quot;) is a sign to split again. The test is whether you can name a number and a reason in ten seconds.</p>\n<p><strong>Mind the stock-flow distinction.</strong> &quot;How many earbuds are in use&quot; is a stock; &quot;how many are sold per year&quot; is a flow. A stock divided by a typical lifetime gives an annual flow, and that is one of the best cross-checks (CS-C04-01).</p>\n<span id=\"c04-c04-5-rounding-precision-and-error-ranges\"></span>\n<h4>C04.5 Rounding, precision and error ranges</h4>\n<p><strong>Rounding.</strong> Round assumptions to one significant figure (&quot;about 3 million&quot;) and the final answer to two at most. Never present more precision than your weakest factor supports. &quot;About 280,000&quot; is better than &quot;283,400,&quot; because the second invites a challenge you cannot answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Ranges, not points.</strong> For each key factor give a low, base and high value, and report the result as &quot;about 280,000, plausibly between 150,000 and 500,000.&quot; If the factors multiply, the extreme range multiplies too (low × low × low to high × high × high), but independent errors partly cancel, so the true range is usually narrower than the extreme range. Treat the multiplied extremes as an outer bound and your base case as the estimate.</p>\n<p><strong>The geometric midpoint.</strong> When a range spans a factor of several, the right center is the geometric mean, not the arithmetic one. For a range of 10 to 40 million, the geometric midpoint is 20 million (the square root of 10 × 40), while the arithmetic midpoint, 25 million, is pulled toward the high end.</p>\n<p><strong>Order of magnitude.</strong> An <strong>order of magnitude</strong> is a factor of ten. <a href=\"#gl-order-of-magnitude\">order of magnitude</a> thinking asks whether an answer is about a thousand, a million or a billion. Most decisions only need you to be within a factor of two or three, and an interviewer will forgive an answer that is a factor of two off but punish one that is a factor of a hundred off with no way to notice <code>Inferred</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Sensitivity analysis.</strong> <strong>Sensitivity analysis</strong> is checking how much the result changes when an assumption changes. <a href=\"#gl-sensitivity-analysis\">sensitivity analysis</a> is simple: move each factor by a factor of two and see which move changes the answer most. That factor is where your remaining uncertainty lives, and it is the one to refine, ask about or test. It is the <a href=\"#gl-pareto-principle\">Pareto principle</a> applied to estimation: one or two factors usually dominate.</p>\n<span id=\"c04-c04-6-estimating-for-a-decision-from-the-number-to-the-so-wh\"></span>\n<h4>C04.6 Estimating for a decision: from the number to the so-what</h4>\n<p>A number matters only if it changes an action. The so-what bridge (FW-C04-05) has four steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Name the decision threshold.</strong> What number would change what we do? &quot;We need at least $50 million of annual revenue to justify a dedicated team&quot; is a threshold. &quot;It's a big market&quot; is not.</li>\n<li><strong>Compare the range with the threshold.</strong> If the whole range is above or below it, the decision is clear. If the range straddles it, the estimate cannot settle the question.</li>\n<li><strong>Find the assumption that drives the straddle,</strong> using sensitivity analysis, and propose the cheapest way to narrow it (a survey, a pilot, a data pull).</li>\n<li><strong>State the action</strong> and what would change it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Good enough is relative to the decision.</strong> A factor-of-two estimate can be entirely sufficient. CS-C04-02 describes a famous case in which a quick estimate that was off by about a factor of two was enough for the decision it was made for.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use the bridge.</strong> When no decision depends on the number, the bridge is unnecessary. In an interview, though, saying &quot;if this were for a launch decision, I'd...&quot; costs a sentence and signals judgment.</p>\n<span id=\"c04-c04-7-the-mental-math-toolkit\"></span>\n<h4>C04.7 The mental-math toolkit</h4>\n<p>Estimation under pressure fails more often on arithmetic nerves than on reasoning. These tools make the arithmetic safe.</p>\n<p><strong>Powers of ten.</strong> Thousand is 10^3, million 10^6, billion 10^9, trillion 10^12. To multiply, add exponents: 3 million × 4 thousand = 12 × 10^9 = 12 billion. Write quantities as a number times a power of ten (4 × 10^5) when they get large.</p>\n<p><strong>Percentages.</strong> 10% is the number with the decimal moved one place; 1% moves it two places. 5% is half of 10%; 15% is 10% plus 5%; 25% is a quarter; 33% a third; 12.5% an eighth. To take 7% of 800: 1% is 8, so 7% is 56.</p>\n<p><strong>Splitting products.</strong> 17 × 24 = 17 × 20 + 17 × 4 = 340 + 68 = 408. Round one factor to a friendly number and correct afterward.</p>\n<p><strong>Time conversions.</strong> Per day to per year, multiply by 365, or by 400 and subtract about 10%. A day has about 10^5 seconds and a year about 3 × 10^7.</p>\n<p><strong>The rule of 72.</strong> Years to double is roughly 72 divided by the growth rate in percent. At 8% a year, doubling takes about 9 years.</p>\n<p><strong>Compounding and percent changes.</strong> A 10% rise followed by a 10% fall leaves you 1% lower, since 1.10 × 0.90 = 0.99. A 50% rise followed by a 50% fall leaves you 25% lower (1.5 × 0.5 = 0.75).</p>\n<p><strong>Bits and bytes.</strong> Eight bits make a byte. One megabit per second is 125 kilobytes per second. One gigabyte is 1,000 megabytes in everyday decimal use, and one petabyte is 1,000 terabytes.</p>\n<p><strong>Units as a check.</strong> Carry the units: people × orders per person per day = orders per day. If the units do not cancel to what you want, a factor is missing.</p>\n<p><strong>Per-capita check.</strong> Divide the result by the population: &quot;that's about two orders per hundred people per day.&quot; Most wrong answers look wrong on a per-capita basis.</p>\n<span id=\"c04-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Six frameworks, set out below. FW-C04-01 is the core protocol, FW-C04-02 and FW-C04-03 supply structure and inputs, FW-C04-04 to FW-C04-06 are the checks. The frame card (FW-C03-01) and the skeleton (FW-C03-07) from C03 are reused for clarifying and pacing.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c04-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C04-01 · The estimation protocol · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a quantity to estimate, a purpose, and ten minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a stated definition, a decomposition, assumptions with provenance, a computed result with a range, a cross-check and a so-what.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the quantity can be decomposed into factors you can reason about; the interviewer will answer clarifying questions and may give corrections.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> speed versus rigor; each extra step costs time and buys confidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> starting with numbers before defining the quantity; unstated assumptions; computing without a cross-check; no link to a decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> &quot;estimate daily food-delivery orders in a city of five million&quot; is defined as orders placed in a day, split by households, share ordering weekly and orders per household, cross-checked from restaurants, and bridged to a courier count at the dinner peak (Worked example 1).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Clarify, Decompose, Assume, Compute, Sanity-check, So-what</strong> (six, counting the bridge).</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c04-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C04-02 · The decomposition library · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a quantity and what you know best about it.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a decomposition with three or four estimable factors, and a second decomposition for the check.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> at least one pattern fits; the factors are roughly uniform within the population.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a simpler pattern is faster and rougher; a richer one catches more but costs time.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> factors that overlap and double count; a factor you cannot estimate; mixing a stock with a flow; ignoring a heavy tail.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the market for an expense tool is estimated top-down from firms, share buying, and price, and checked bottom-up from the vendors' customer counts (Worked example 2).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The seven patterns are in the table in C04.4: top-down, bottom-up, balance, capacity, benchmark, unit economics, funnel.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c04-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C04-03 · The anchor ladder · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a factor that needs a number.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a value, a stated provenance (D, P or J), a confidence level and a range.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can reason to a plausible value even without an exact figure.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a published figure is more credible and may be wrong or out of date; a judgment is flexible and can be biased.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> presenting a judgment as a fact; quoting a &quot;fact&quot; you are not sure of; anchoring on one memorized figure; failing to give a range.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> for the share of adults with a smartphone, the ladder gives a judgment (J) of 85%, stated as an assumption with a plausible range of 70% to 95% (Worked example 1 and the Baseline, Strong, Standout answers).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The ladder, from most to least preferred:</strong> (1) a defined fact (D); (2) a widely published figure you are fairly sure of (P), stated as approximate; (3) a figure derived from your own experience, anchored to something you can name (J); (4) a plain guess, which you say is a guess and show a range for. Always say which rung you are on.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c04-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C04-04 · The two-route sanity check · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a computed estimate and a second decomposition.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a verdict (the routes agree within a factor of about two, or they do not), and a note on which assumption explains any gap.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the two routes are independent enough that agreement means something.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a second route costs a minute and catches large errors; two routes that share an assumption can agree and both be wrong.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a &quot;second route&quot; that reuses the same assumptions; stopping at agreement without a unit and per-capita check; ignoring a large gap.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> top-down daily deliveries of about 86,000 are compared with a bottom-up figure of 80,000 from restaurant counts, and the two agree within 10% (Worked example 1).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Checks, in order: (1) units; (2) per-capita or per-unit plausibility; (3) a second independent route; (4) a bound (can it be larger than the population? smaller than one?); (5) a known comparison (is it close to something I know?). If routes disagree by more than about a factor of two, find which assumption differs and decide which you trust more.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c04-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C04-05 · The so-what bridge · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> an estimate with a range, and the decision it serves.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a decision threshold, a verdict (clear above, clear below, or straddling), the driving assumption, a cheap way to narrow it, and an action.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the decision can be stated with a number that would change it.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a precise threshold forces clarity and can be arbitrary; sensitivity work costs time.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> no threshold (&quot;it's big&quot;); treating a range that straddles the threshold as if it settled the question; proposing a study that costs more than the decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> an AI feature's daily cost converts to a break-even conversion rate of about 3%, which the team compares with a plausible range for paid conversion (Worked example 4).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: threshold, compare, drive, narrow, act (C04.6).</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c04-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C04-06 · The range method · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> an estimate built from factors with uncertain values.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a base case, a low case and a high case, a geometric midpoint, and the factor that drives the spread.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> factors are roughly independent; low and high values are chosen as plausible, not extreme.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> ranges are more honest and take longer; multiplied extremes overstate the true spread.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> extreme values that make the range useless; arithmetic midpoints for wide ranges; reporting only the base case.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> for riders in a city of ten million, the share of adults who ride on a given day is taken as 3% to 6%, giving 210,000 to 420,000 riders with a base of about 280,000 (Baseline, Strong, Standout answers in section 6).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: (1) base value for each factor; (2) plausible low and high for the two or three most uncertain; (3) compute low, base, high; (4) take the geometric midpoint if the range is wide; (5) report &quot;about X, plausibly Y to Z.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c04-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<p>Four question types use this chapter. Spend the first minutes on structure and keep time for the check and the so-what, which most candidates skip.</p>\n<span id=\"c04-type-a-the-pure-estimate-how-many-x\"></span>\n<h4>Type A: the pure estimate (&quot;How many X?&quot;)</h4>\n<p><em>Example prompts:</em> &quot;Estimate the number of coffee shops in a city of two million.&quot; &quot;How many video calls happen at a company of 10,000 people each day?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Time budget for a ten-minute segment</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clarify</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n<td>Definition, scope, unit, stock or flow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decompose</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n<td>Three or four factors from the library</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assume</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>A number, a reason and a provenance for each</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compute</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Stepwise arithmetic with units</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sanity-check</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n<td>A second route, a per-capita check, the dominant assumption</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>So-what and close</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n<td>The range, the threshold, what you would do next</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>What to clarify first.</strong> The definition of the thing (what counts), the geography and the time frame, and whether the answer is a stock or a flow. <strong>Where to signpost.</strong> &quot;I'll split this into four factors.&quot; &quot;Now I'll cross-check from the other side.&quot; <strong>How to close.</strong> &quot;About 1,250 shops, plausibly between 800 and 2,500. The share of people who buy coffee out dominates; if this were for a store-opening decision I'd check that first.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c04-type-b-market-sizing-and-opportunity-how-big-is-the-opportun\"></span>\n<h4>Type B: market sizing and opportunity (&quot;How big is the opportunity?&quot;)</h4>\n<p><em>Example prompts:</em> &quot;Estimate the annual revenue for a mobile wallet in a country of 50 million adults.&quot; &quot;How big is the market for expense software for small businesses?&quot;</p>\n<p>Use top-down or funnel with the unit being customers, then multiply by price. Separate the <strong>total</strong> market (everyone who could use it) from the <strong>serviceable</strong> market (those you can reach with your product and channels) and from the <strong>obtainable</strong> share. Name the decision threshold early (what size justifies action), and finish with the so-what bridge. A strong answer says which of the three (total, serviceable, obtainable) the number is. The market-sizing vocabulary and its reality checks are developed in C18.</p>\n<span id=\"c04-type-c-capacity-cost-and-technical-estimates-how-much-storag\"></span>\n<h4>Type C: capacity, cost and technical estimates (&quot;How much storage, how many servers, what cost?&quot;)</h4>\n<p><em>Example prompts:</em> &quot;How much storage does a photo-sharing app need in a year?&quot; &quot;How much capacity does a live stream with five million concurrent viewers require?&quot;</p>\n<p>Use capacity and throughput. Pay particular attention to units (bits and bytes, megabytes and terabytes), to peak versus average, and to redundancy (copies, headroom). Convert to cost with a stated unit price, flagged as an assumption. Finish with what the number means for a decision: cost per user, break-even, or which design choice matters most. Technical background is in C21, system-design questions in C22, and AI cost estimates in C24 and C25.</p>\n<span id=\"c04-type-d-critique-a-number-our-market-is-10-billion-do-you-bel\"></span>\n<h4>Type D: critique a number (&quot;Our market is $10 billion. Do you believe it?&quot;)</h4>\n<p>Ask for the decomposition behind the number. Rebuild it from the bottom, factor by factor. Look for the usual errors: a total market presented as a serviceable one, a share-of-a-big-number argument (&quot;just 1% of a trillion&quot;), a price applied to customers who will not pay it, and factors that overlap. Say which factor drives the gap, and give your own range. Worked in IQ-C04-06.</p>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules for all types.</strong> Go broad before deep: choose the structure before computing. Keep arithmetic visible. If you make an arithmetic error, say so and fix it; interviewers value the recovery. If the interviewer corrects an assumption, update the whole chain aloud and say how much the answer changes. When the clock runs short, give the estimate and the range first, then the check, rather than finishing the computation and having no conclusion.</p>\n<span id=\"c04-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All four examples are <code>Illustrative</code>: the products are fictional, and every figure that is not a defined fact is an invented assumption, marked as such.</p>\n<span id=\"c04-worked-example-1-consumer-daily-food-delivery-orders-in-a-ci\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — Consumer: daily food-delivery orders in a city of five million (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>Clarify.</strong> An order is one checkout, not one item. &quot;Per day&quot; is a flow. The city is five million people (invented). The purpose is courier planning, so the answer should also give the dinner peak.</p>\n<p><strong>Decompose (top-down).</strong> Orders per day = households × share that order delivery weekly × orders per active household per week ÷ 7.</p>\n<p><strong>Assume, with provenance.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Factor</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n<th>Class</th>\n<th>Range</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>People per household</td>\n<td>2.5</td>\n<td>P (typical for a high-income city)</td>\n<td>2.2 to 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Households</td>\n<td>5,000,000 ÷ 2.5 = 2,000,000</td>\n<td>computed</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Share ordering at least weekly</td>\n<td>20%</td>\n<td>J (invented)</td>\n<td>10% to 30%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Orders per active household per week</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n<td>J (invented)</td>\n<td>1.2 to 2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n";
